e six new  reagents highlighted in bold  and so you can see really for any  channel on a flow cytometer  there's a  probably a fixable viability die that  can be used or that can be added to  a multi-color  immunophenotyping experiment that you're  conducting  and as we can see regardless of the  color the distinction between live in uh  cells in the dim peak and dead cells in  the bright peak uh is similar we just  have the opportunity now to  install this viability indicator  in  whatever part of the spectrum  best suits your experiment  and so as part of this launch of six new  dyes  we have  gone back and looked at the workflow  looked at the protocol required  for these reagents  and so i'll show you  briefly some of some of the expanded  characterization we've done  to really study these dyes now first  the the protocol that exists with the  current live dead fixable dies includes  a low throughput  protocol  and realizing that  that today many researchers are not just  using a few tubes but are actually doing  medium throughput or  very high throughput experiments  we've expanded the the protocol included  with these kits to include  both of these methods now these are  similar in the fact that  the first step is dissolving the  dry dye in dmso  and then essentially making a one to one  thousand dilution onto cells  uh then carrying out an incubation step  um and then after that um i'll show you  dave's just to support this  but everything beyond this is um we can  consider optional and so it is  recommended to do  a wash step after labeling your cells  then it's possible to label  with antibodies against surface antigens  then fix and perm if you prefer  and then label with antibodies against  intracellular targets followed by  analysis but again everything in this  box is  is now optional for these  six new dies  on the medium throughput method  essentially we do the same thing we  dissolve our dye in dmso  and then depending on the number of  tubes that you need to label  just dilute this die one to one thousand  into pbs  spin down your cells and bring them back  up in one milliliter of this  ready-to-use working dye solution per  tube  so this helps you scale a bit more  quickly  for 96 or 384 well plates  essentially just spin down the plate and  remove the media from your cells and add  the working solution of dye to every  well and proceed with the rest of the  protocol  so these these reunions can all very  easily be applied to higher throughput  experiments  now i mentioned in the previous  slide  that  among the things that are optional  one of these is washing  and this this is somewhat surprising but  the data does does support this  conclusion that whether we wash  ourselves or we don't  whether we just label the cells and  immediately analyze them without washing  away unbound dye  we're still quite easily able to resolve  live and dead cells and in some cases as  in this near ir 876 example there's  really no difference between washed or  unwashed cells  for absolute best results it is still  recommended to wash but  in cases where your cells may be  sensitive  to you know centrifugation for example  it's it's not critical to  wash your cells  another interesting uh conclusion from  our additional characterization  experiments  is that  you do do you need to label your cells  in pbs alone or can you label cells with  some protein present such as  bovine serum albumin  and we can say  uh once again somewhat surprisingly that  we can label ourselves in  a buffer such as phosphate buffered  saline with one percent  bsa  and once again in in some cases there  may be a slight change in  the the signal or the background or both  but in in most cases there's very little  change  due to labeling in one percent bsa  and so in many cases this will save  you know a centrifugation step during  your workflow if your cells are already  in a fax buffer such as pbs with bsa you  can just go ahead and label them and  carry on with the protocol without a  buffer change  and  in the case of um you know complete  media such as 10 bsa i would recommend  using  either the near ir 780 or the lime 506  these both seem to be compatible with  protein concentration up to 10  uh with the other dyes we will expect  some some loss of signal with that much  protein present  but one percent is is compatible with  all six of these dyes  so another another  question that i often get from from  scientists is  if i label my cells now and fix and perm  them do i have to analyze them  immediately or is it okay to save them  and analyze them tomorrow or  you know on monday after after the  weekend  and so in in this experiment  i  labeled cells with four different dies  fixed and firmed them with the  ebioscience ic fix and perm kit and  analyze them and then put those samples  in the refrigerator in this case for 35  days  and analyze the same samples again  and so as you can see that distinction  between live and dead cells is very well  preserved because these dyes covalently  bind to proteins  within or on the surface of cells  and that  is essentially  largely unchanged over the course of a  month and so  i'd say  if you want to wait until tomorrow when  your  flow cytometer is available or if it's  friday afternoon and you want to wait  till monday  to analyze  the fixable viability dies are fine with  that  you might want to confirm  with the rest of your antibody panel if  it's  compatible with that but there's no  concern about losing  a distinction between life and dead  cells over time  now  another  potential  source of concern is are these dyes that  i'm putting on my cells going to be  toxic  in themselves that is  will these have some impact on the  viability of my cells just by labeling  them and so to to answer this  using the six  new dyes that we're introducing  i labeled a population of cells with  each of those and then put them back in  an incubator for 24 hours  and compared them to just dmso alone  and then using another class of  viability dyes called cytox dyes either  cytox red or cytotox blue  depending on the color requirements  we can see in every case our  cells have the same background  level of cell death which is about five  percent so there's really no change  between those cells labeled with dmso  alone for 24 hours or or  with dmso alone washed and then  incubated for 24 hours or those labeled  with any of these dyes and so we can say  that  these dyes are not  going to reduce more  cell death in our cells of interest  now a final  workflow example when you're  wanting to create single color controls  for your multi-color immuno phenotype  experiments  um each of these dyes is compatible with  our arc amine reactive compensation  beads  and with this um  this  bead product essentially we put one drop  of beads into a  cytometry tube and label it  with  whichever color of dye we'd like a  single color control for  after washing we had a negative bead and  we have two peaks that can be used to  very easily set compensation  for  these dyes without requiring uh the use  of of your cells um and so um  as i as i mentioned at the beginning  um these  new dies that we're introducing are  designed to fill channels where live  dead fixable dies did not exist  previously  and also they're  screened so that they have minimal  spillover into adjacent  channels on a flow cytometer and so for  example  using just the yellow laser on an  attuned nxt  flow cytometer  we can use the live dead fixable orange  in our  yl1 channel to get on live cells in this  case these are human pbmcs so we'll get  on live cells and get on lymphocytes  and then we can use  two additional markers sorry three  additional antibodies um  which are all pe tandems uh to  conduct a multicolored amino phenotyping  experiment  and as we can see  there is compensation required of course  but once applied we can readily  distinguish  positive and negative populations of  cells  and so in this case with four dyes all  on the same laser  we have now left the rest of our entire  flow cytometer available for  additional  markers  so  um a few uh highlights we discussed uh  and actually one that we didn't discuss  is we'd recommend labeling with these  dies for 20 minutes  to get the brightest signal  but in fact in in all cases two minutes  is often sufficient to get clear  distinction between  uh live and dead cells  so if your cells are very sensitive um  to being in the labeling environment  this is often sufficient but we would  recommend  for the best signal to continue for the  full 20 minutes  you can fix in perm but it's not  required  we also recommend washing after labeling  but again that's not required either  so if you  do want to sort your cells  this can be conducted as one of the last  steps before that  sorting  so let's move on now and we'll discuss  some applications  areas or experiments where  the addition of these dyes  brings to bring some value to you  now often  with a fixable viability die  you may not see just a positive and a  negative peak especially if you're using  blood cells  as in life's whole blood  or peripheral blood mononuclear cells  and in some cases we may see three peaks  so to help decide which peak we should  gate on  we can set a gate on the different  populations and then  back gate onto our scatter plot and see  where these are  in this case our dimmest peak is our  lymphocytes  this intermediate peak is in fact our  live monocytes while these two brighter  peaks are the live and dead lymphocytes  and so if we're interested in  uh immunophytic immunophenotyping all of  our cells we would probably expand this  gate to  include both of the populations on the  left  if we're interested just in lymphocytes  maybe  the gate on the left is is sufficient  but in fact a back gate will help answer  which which peak should you gate on  now another  tool that will help answer this question  is a new flow cytometer called the  attune  sight pix cytometer  now this is the same  a tuned nxt flow cytometer with the  added benefit of bright field images of  every cell  that you have acquired  and so we can now  set a gate on what we think is our live  cells  look at a scatter plot and see that  in fact these cells are  where we would expect live cells to be  by scatter  we now look at  three representative images of cells  within this gate we see they're round  and uniform and smooth  uh and and  as live cells should be  we now gain on the very bright positive  peak and back gate to our scatter plot  these are where we'd expect dead cells  to be  and we can see why the side scatter of  this population is higher they're quite  granular they have  some irregularity and some texture  inside  and so this is what our dead cells look  like  now in this experiment there was a third  peak in our viability histogram  if we get here and back gate we see  these are dead cells but also  what might be called you know beyond  dead these are starting to lose their  structural integrity and fall apart in  that case components of the cell that  have been labeled with our viability dye  are being lost and so  on a fluorescent  basis we'd expect these cells to be  dimmer and that's why this population is  less bright than the rest of the dead  cells  and so this is really why we see three  peaks in this example  now  the attuned sight picks instrument can  also be used to show  the the interesting morphology  of yeast cells  as determined uh to be live or dead  with live dead fixable orange viability  stain so if we get on our negative peak  which is our  live yeast cells we see that these are  in general a uniform circular shape but  in in some cases we see buds emerging  from the cells and this indicates that  these cells are live and actively  proliferating  and via budding they're creating new  yeast  those cells in the positive peak we know  are dead and with the bright field  images provided by the sitefix  instrument we can see there's really  irregular morphology here  that creates higher side scatter and in  fact we can confirm  not only that these are our singlets but  that they are  morphologically they look dead  so um  we can also look at other examples  for example in  immunotherapy  if we're studying chimeric antigen  receptor t cells uh very often these  cells after being produced are  cryopreserved  for shipment or storage  and upon thawing  we'd expect to see  a fair amount of dead cells within  our entire cell population  and so by scatter we can see this has  two populations which  we could say are roughly live and dead  by scatter  but as i demonstrated previously a  scattergate really isn't conclusive  we need the distinction between live and  dead cells  as indicated by a viability dye  and so here we have some car t cells  labeled with each of these six dies and  showing us a clear distinction between  those live and  dead cells  and so  we can use that distinction between live  and dead on an instrument such as the  in vitrogen bigfoot spectral cell sorter  to show us a couple of things one  we can visualize the entire  spectral emission profile of this dye  on a seven laser  bigfoot  cell sorter  but if we limit our  view to just one  fluorescent histogram of a single  channel in this case i'm excited at 561  with emission at 575 we can now gate on  live and dead cells  and actually go a step further and sort  out those live cells and  remove the dead cells from our car t  population  so in this example especially it can be  quite valuable to  remove  the dead cells from our entire  population of car t cells and only carry  on with our uh research or our analysis  on live car t cells  so if we can uh look within our entire  emission spectrum  uh to one specific channel and display  that a different way as a histogram  we can now sort out  the cells in the negative peak  and see that these are the live car t  cells well our dead cells in this case  we captured them but we can  just remove our dead cells from the  mixed culture  so  if if analysis is sufficient  each of these six new dyes is also  compatible with  other  full-spectrum cytometry instruments so  we can have a clear distinction between  live and dead cells  using whatever  spectral analysis instrument  is available to us  and while these dyes are intended for  flow cytometry uh of course they are  they provide very bright dead cells and  very dim  fluorescents in live cells uh this  distinction is also visible by using  fluorescence microscopy  and so for example using the evo m5000  imaging system we can swap out the  color of  the  led light cubes within this instrument  to match the  spectral requirements of any of these  dyes  and so we can see  with three different examples that our  dead cells are very brightly fluorescent  throughout the cell  while our live cells  have an absence of fluorescence  and so this this distinction is actually  visible  with fluorescence microscopy as well i  will give the caveat that these are  optimized for flow cytometry and so with  with microscopy  a couple of these dyes may have  photostability issues  and  with the very long stoke shift of some  of the live dead fixable dies it can be  challenging  to find a microscope that is compatible  with  the unusual  spectra of these dyes but the led light  cubes  contained within the evos imaging system  can also be adapted  to the countess 3fl automated cell  counter and so with this instrument we  can take 10 microliters of our cell  sample put it into a slide and  ask this instrument to give us not only  the concentration of our cells but  actually the percent  dead in this case so in this sample we  took  10 microliters of our car t cells  um  labeled with our lifetime fixable lime  506 viability die  and asked the countess  to give us the percent viable in this  case we have 60 dead cells and our cell  concentration  is 7 times 10 to the fifth per ml and we  actually have an image  where it it shows us which cells it's  counting is live and which are being  counted as dead  so  i want to thank everyone for  joining us today  i hope these new reagents will bring  value to  your flow cytometry experiments and  hopefully give you some new ideas uh for  what's possible  not only from a reagent perspective but  also uh with some of the really amazing  new flow cytometry instruments that are  available so thank you for your time  thank you for your time  and thank you chris for that informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion of this webinar and if you  have any questions you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on that ask  a question box located on the far left  of your screen and we'll answer as many  as we have time for  so  chris our first questions coming already  in from our great audience is  i would like to add a live dead fixable  viability  die to my spectral flow panel  can you provide any more guidance on how  to design this panel  yeah definitely thanks for the the  question  um and this is a key  uh concern now that we're seeing a lot  of research pivot from conventional  cytometry into spectral space  and more specifically wondering how  these dyes can be optimized to to  perform on a spectral  cytometer so first i just want to  mention that  we have some great guidance available on  our  website on thermofisher.com there's a  full  spectral cytometry page  with guidance about designing panels for  spectral cytometry  um  and also with our  spectroviewer the the excitation and  emission spectra of all of these dyes  are available  from 300 out to 900 nanometers so you  can really get a good idea of  where  some of these die show up in different  channels  and also just to just to mention  um as we developed each of these live  dead fixable  viability dyes  we  package them at a concentration where  you get the the best discrimination  between live and dead 