n their live cells  and then  continuing on to gate on their  various antibody markers in their panel  and live cells  we know are dim and so have very minimal  spillover into adjacent channels so with  these dyes we're not so much concerned  about  uh the spillover of these dyes into  to other channels but i also see a  a good follow-up question to this that  has has just shown up and actually how  we can compensate these  sure you did you want to go ahead and  and answer that question the question is  from our audience  how to accomplish how do we compensate  of those live dead die  yeah um  thank you susie and i think um  there are a couple of ways to compensate  these live dead dies and you know  compensation becomes critical as we get  into these larger panels with many  markers in many colors so i showed one  way to set compensation and that's using  our arc  amine reactive compensation beads that  are designed  to be labeled with amine reactive dye  such as these  and then just run as a single color  control so we can use compensation beads  for our antibodies and we can use  similar compensation beads  for our  viability dyes  however as we begin to look at  spectral cytometry  sometimes  beads are not preferred sometimes they  are  if if you prefer not to use beads to set  compensation for for any reason  it's also possible to take a sample of  your cells and kill them i generally  just use  50 or 60 degrees celsius  uh for 10 to 20 minutes depending on how  sturdy your cells are  so that you can get a nice population of  dead cells uh then we'll just label  those with the viability dye and that  will give us a nice bright uh population  to get on  and this is often a preferred method for  spectral cytometry so that we can have  our cells be consistent across all of  our  controls  so yeah i think thanks for the question  and thank you chris and i want to thank  our audience for these great questions  we have a couple more our next audience  member says cou